// SLIP, RFC 1055 -- Serial Line IP.
//
// The smallest framing protocol worth modelling, and included because it is
// the one that makes the case for `\xNN`: every constant SLIP is built from
// is a byte no character spells. END is 0xC0, ESC is 0xDB, and the two
// substitutes are 0xDC and 0xDD.
//
// It is also the shape most unlike the other examples here. There is no
// header, no length field and no checksum -- nothing to read a number out
// of. A frame is "bytes until END", and the only reason that is unambiguous
// is the stuffing: an END inside the payload is sent as ESC ESC_END, so the
// END that ends the frame cannot occur anywhere else. The framing and the
// codec are the same fact, which is why the region carries both.

target buffer;
endian big;
bit_order msb_first;

// One byte out per byte in, except for the two that cannot travel as
// themselves, which take two. `worst_case = 2, per = 1` is a payload of
// nothing but END or ESC.
//
// The substitution is a table rather than a transformation: END becomes 0xDC
// and ESC becomes 0xDD, and neither is one operation applied to the other.
// That is why the generated decoder refuses an escape sequence the code does
// not define instead of computing something from it -- there is nothing to
// compute, and guessing would invent a byte. PPP is the same shape with the
// opposite answer: its escape is defined as exclusive-or with 0x20, so its
// decoder reverses escapes nobody enumerated. Both are in `std/kernels.situ`.
codec slip {
	kernel = stuffing(worst_case = 2, per = 1, unit = byte, code = slip);
}
impl slip derived;

struct frame {
	// The whole of it. `until "\xC0"` is the framing and `(slip)` is what
	// makes the framing sound: the scan runs on the *encoded* bytes, where
	// END appears exactly once and at the end, which is the property the
	// stuffing exists to create.
	//
	// The map records the consequence: the region is `seekable = none` and
	// its expansion is `ratio_bounded(2, 1)`, so nothing inside it can be
	// addressed without decoding first. That is true of every stuffing code
	// and is why they belong at the outermost layer.
	coded datagram(slip) until "\xC0" {
		u8 payload[remaining];
	}
}

// The frame has no header, so the payload begins at byte zero and always
// does -- there is nothing in front of it whose size could vary. That is the
// one static fact SLIP offers, and stating it is what makes the map's
// `offset=AbsoluteStatic(0x00)` a claim somebody checks rather than an
// observation somebody made.
require absolute_static(frame.datagram);

// What situ does not describe here, said plainly.
//
// RFC 1055 recommends sending an END *before* a frame as well, so that line
// noise before a transmission is flushed as an empty frame the receiver
// discards. That is a sender's convention and a receiver's tolerance, not a
// property of the bytes: a leading END produces a zero-length frame, which
// this schema reads as a frame with an empty payload rather than as nothing.
// A reader that wants RFC 1055's behaviour discards those itself.
//
// There is also no datagram size limit here. RFC 1055 names 1006 bytes as
// what an implementation must accept, which is a requirement on the peer
// rather than a fact about the encoding, and a schema that stated it would
// be refusing frames the specification permits.
